# Same names as the Parameter Store keys under /argon/<env>/
# In AWS every environment points at the Postgres container on the instance, host `postgres`, with
# no TLS: the connection never leaves the Compose network.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://argon:argon@localhost:5432/argon_dev
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
INTERNAL_API_SECRET=local-dev-secret-change-me
# Derives the unsubscribe token of every subscriber (src/subscriber/token.ts). At least 32 characters;
# rotating it breaks every unsubscribe link already sent. Generate: openssl rand -base64 48
UNSUBSCRIBE_TOKEN_SECRET=local-dev-unsubscribe-secret-change-me-too

# change to production values in AWS
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3001

# The clock: the schedules in the `mastra` schema (generation 5h30, send 7h, watch 8h, retention 4h,
# America/Sao_Paulo) fire only where this is on. With it off the rows are still created and shown in
# the Studio, and a run is still one POST /pipeline/run, or the Run button of a workflow, away.
# Turn it on locally to test a schedule: a mocked run costs nothing (src/pipeline/schedules.ts).
SCHEDULER_ENABLED=false

# Mastra Studio served by this API under /studio, and the traces it shows. On in dev and lab, off in
# prod. Locally it is http://localhost:3001/studio; save the x-internal-secret header once in the
# Studio's settings (instance http://localhost:3001, prefix /mastra).
STUDIO_ENABLED=false

# Where this is: local | lab | dev | prod. It decides how much a run may cost — the model, how far
# the agent may search, and whether it runs at all or over the fixture (src/pipeline/profile.ts).
# The deploy writes it in AWS; here it is the cheapest profile, which collects and writes nothing
# from a model. `POST /pipeline/run {"mode":"live"}` pays for a real run when you need one.
ARGON_ENV=local

# Absolute origins that go into the e-mail links: the site serves the unsubscribe page, the API
# the one-click endpoint (RFC 8058). In AWS, https://<env> and https://api.<env>.
#
# The site is https even locally: the edition validation only accepts https links, and a link the
# local site could not answer would be worse than no link. `pnpm certs` makes the certificate and
# `pnpm dev` serves it. The API stays http — it is reached only by the site and by curl on this
# machine, it never appears in the validated HTML, and putting it behind a private authority would
# only break the call the site makes (Next does not pass NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS to its own server).
WEB_ORIGIN=https://localhost:3000
API_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3001

# Where the e-mail images come from (src/email/assets.ts). Leave it unset: here they come from the
# site (WEB_ORIGIN + /email), and in lab, dev and prod from the public bucket under the environment's
# prefix, which the deploy fills (deploy/aws/README.md, "Arquivos públicos"). Nothing to add to
# Parameter Store. Set it only to point every e-mail somewhere else, e.g. the dev prefix of the bucket
# to see locally what dev serves:
# PUBLIC_ASSETS_ORIGIN=https://argon-public-382597877834.s3.sa-east-1.amazonaws.com/dev

# Mail. The default is the local Mailpit (pnpm db:up), which accepts everything and delivers
# nothing: http://localhost:8025. In AWS, MAIL_TRANSPORT=resend plus the key.
MAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp
SMTP_URL=smtp://localhost:1025
RESEND_API_KEY=
